olina's slight Republican lean and the neutral open-seat dynamic. Early primary activity offers no reliable signal about how the state would vote once nominees are set.", "watch": "General-election polling in North Carolina once both nominees are set, especially whether the Republican nominee holds the state's narrow lean.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "North Carolina remains one of the closest presidential battlegrounds, and the 2028 open-seat race has almost no race-specific evidence yet. The state's recent partisan lean favors Republicans by about a point, and with the term-limited president leaving office, neither side gets an incumbency advantage. No general-election polls, expert ratings, or market prices are available for this race, so the forecast rests on the state's partisan lean alone.\n\nEarly attention has focused on the primaries: national surveys and the CPAC straw poll show Vance leading the Republican field, with Buttigieg narrowly ahead among Democrats, but none of this measures a North Carolina general election. Until nominees are chosen and state polling arrives, the race is best treated as a near coin-flip leaning very slightly Republican, with unusually wide uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4508, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4639, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.4659, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.4636, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.4637, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.4628, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.4642, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.4667, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.4617, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "202